From b1d7bce6a3d70651f3c518011c1359c1176e338e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Creeper23456 <Creeper23456@outlook.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 13:48:32 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] P18: the CGS daemon has its own names: CGS_DAEMON_*
 environment variables and a __cgs_worker_daemon selector

Reason: A machine can run stock omp and cgs side by side. Both daemons carried the same names (`__omp_worker_daemon` on the command line, `OMP_DAEMON_DIR`, `OMP_DAEMON_TCP`, `OMP_DAEMON_RESUME` in the environment), so a pattern match (`pkill -f omp_worker_daemon`), an inherited `OMP_DAEMON_DIR` from a stock omp shell, or a stray `OMP_DAEMON_TCP` pointed one product's restart (or its clients) at the other's daemon. cgs launches its daemon in its own directory under its own names, so restarting it never touches omp's.
Upstream: none (distribution-specific names)
Remove-when: CGS stops shipping a branded omp build

- daemon/protocol.ts: DAEMON_WORKER_ARG is `__cgs_worker_daemon`; DAEMON_DIR_ENV, DAEMON_TCP_ENV and DAEMON_RESUME_ENV are `CGS_DAEMON_DIR`, `CGS_DAEMON_TCP`, `CGS_DAEMON_RESUME`. Every use goes through these constants, so client, server and the CLI selector stay in step
- pi-utils worker-host.ts: isWorkerHostSelector also accepts the `__cgs_worker_` prefix. Without it the renamed daemon is not recognized as a worker, starts as a full CLI, and spawns the daemon again without end
- the two test-only grace knobs (`OMP_DAEMON_*_GRACE_MS`) and the Rust IPC endpoint stem are unchanged: the endpoint lives under the daemon directory, which cgscli pins to ~/.cgs/run/daemon through CGS_DAEMON_DIR
- tests and fixtures that set the old names follow
